ing beyond the moon and the stars. I love (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And you're wearing the stars because you are a star, Tory.\par \par \uc2 TORY JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) There you go. I love you past the moon and the stars. There's so many meanings to these pieces. I love them from Sterling Forever, so get your ear stacks, get your celestial pieces today. Huge savings, all of these pieces $10 to $30.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STERLING FOREVER, $10 - $30 FOR GMA\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's a great deal.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PUFFIN DRINK WEAR, $20 - $25 ORIGINAL\par \par \uc2 TORY JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So good. Okay, this is making its "gma Deals" debut. It's Puffin, and look at this, it's drink wear for your cans. So, see how cute that is? It's literally made with insulation just like they would make outer wear. The bottom is nonstick. This is fun. Even Sal gave it a thumbs up. Keeps your drinks colder longer. I love these. Slashed in half, starts at $10.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PUFFIN DRINK WEAR, $10 - $12.50 FOR GMA\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) These look like Cowboys and Eagles. We can get those out of here.\par \par \uc2 TORY JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Sorry.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All of these are available, America.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SWIG, $13 - $50 ORIGINAL\par \par \uc2 TORY JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) There you go. And then Swig. My favorite drinkware. Look at the size of this. This is the first time ever we have their 40 ounce mega tumbler.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I like this. I need one of these.\par \par \uc2 TORY JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That is so fabulous. We've got so many different patterns, all insulated. These are great for hot or cold drinks. Coffee, cocktails, wine or water, your choice. They start at $6.50.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SWIG, $6.50 - $25 FOR GMA\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You had me at cocktails. All right, thank you so much, Tory. And we've partnered with these companies on these deals. You can get them on our website. Tory's birthday, everybody. We've got to celebrate that, two days in a row. She's so special to us here at "GMA." And coming up, we're cruising into the weekend with a big surprise.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STORY SYNOPSES\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020231002ej9f0000p\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 AUTOWORKERS GO ON STRIKE HISTORIC WALKOUT AFTER NO DEAL REACHED WITH BIG THREE CARMAKERS\b0\par\par\uc2 705 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 September 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NO DEAL\par \par \uc2 STRIKE, AUTOWORKERS, CAR MAKERS, FAIN, FARLEY\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The union is taking unprecedented action against all big three automakers at once. But only walking out at one plant for each company. Senior national correspondent Terry Moran is outside a Ford plant in Michigan with the latest. Good morning, Terry.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AUTOWORKERS GO ON STRIKE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HISTORIC WALKOUT AFTER NO DEAL REACHED WITH BIG THREE CARMAKERS\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, George. Yeah, I'm here outside the Ford plant in Wayne where they make Broncos and Rangers, and where, as you can see, they've been on the picket line since Midnight last night. Hundreds last night. Now they're going in shifts. And it's really been a summer of strikes, hasn't it? In Hollywood, health care, UPS, and now the big one, the auto industry. The \uc2 UAW\uc2  demanding, like the others, better pay and benefits, and trying to shift the economy back towards workers.\par \par \uc2 AUTOWORKER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Union.\par \par \uc2 AUTOWORKERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Union.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) At the stroke of Midnight and a deal nowhere in sight, the United Autoworkers are now on a historic strike, for the first time walking out of facilities from all of the big three US automakers.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN (PRESIDENT \uc2 UAW\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  is ready to stand up. This is our defining moment.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But this is not a mass walkout, for now. The union is aiming to disrupt corporate operations with targeted strikes at three facilities, a Ford plant in Michigan, a GM plant in Missouri, and an Ohio plant for Chrysler's owner \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 . The union says a total of 12,700 workers have walked off the job so far.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN (PRESIDENT \uc2 UAW\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 This strategy will keep the companies guessing.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In the final hours a behind the scenes scramble to avoid what would be a devastating strike, with President Biden calling \uc2 UAW\uc2  President Shawn Fain and all three of the automakers leader. And Ford and \uc2 GM\uc2  offering a 20% wage increase, but that's only half of what the union was calling for, a 40% increase, they say that's right in line with the pay increases the CEO's have had since the last contract.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Workers also demanding a return to pension plans, better retiree healthcare and cost of living adjustments. Ford's CEO telling us that the union's demands are unsustainable in an industry aiming to adapt to the future.\par \par \uc2 JIM FARLEY\uc2  (CEO FORD)\par \par \uc2 We are one of the most important industries for jobs in America. Everyone will suffer, especially in the communities where we have plants.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) By one estimate, a strike at all three companies could cost the economy more than $5 billion, with tens of thousands of jobs lost. But for Scott Fox out on the picket lines here this morning, this strike is about much more than the auto industry.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So bottom line, people watching this, what do you want people to know about what you're doing out here?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT FOX (MEMBER \uc2 UAW\uc2  LOCAL 600)\par \par \uc2 I want them to know that we're looking out for everybody, not just the union people here at this plant. You got the mega dollar dudes, and you got us down here.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The companies say they have put a fair offer on the table and they say they are prepared for a strike. They've got inventory of cars and some money in the bank, and this could go on for a while. But as you heard the from Scott Fox, for the workers this is something more, this is a time to reset the balance between workers and management, which they believe has been out of whack for a long, long time. George.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, Terry, thanks very much. We'll see how it plays out. \uc2 GM\uc2 's CEO Mary Barro is going to join us live in our next hour. Michael.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020231002ej9f00002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 AUTOWORKERS GO ON STRIKE \uc2 GENERAL MOTORS\uc2  CEO ON \uc2 UAW\uc2  WALKOUT AND THE NEGOTIATIONS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,441 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 September 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NO DEAL\par \par \uc2 STRIKE, AUTOWORKERS, CAR MAKERS, FAIN, FARLEY\par \par \uc2 LEE, EAST COAST, WIND, RIP CURRENTS, WAVES\par \par \uc2 LEE, ATLANTIC, WINDS, BEACHES\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, INDICTED, DOJ, GUN CHARGES, GARLAND, GOP, DEMS\par \par \uc2 LOWELL, BIDEN, INDICTMENT, ADDICTION, PLEA, POLITICS\par \par \uc2 LIBYA, FLOOD, DAMS, DEATHS, MISSING\par \par \uc2 CAVALCANTE, CAPTURED, PRISON, SECURITY, YODA\par \par \uc2 SANDERS, COLORADO, FOOTBALL, RIVAL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WIND FORECAST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 MURDAUGH, PROSECUTORS, TRAIL, HILL, JURORS, GRIFFIN, BLAND\par \par \uc2 CYBERATTACK, MGM, CAESARS, SHUTDOWN, CASINOS, STAMOS\par \par \uc2 TUOHY, OHER, CONSERVATORSHIP, "BLIND SIDE", LAWSUIT\par \par \uc2 MORTGAGE, RATES, ASSUMABLE, TIKTOK, TRAPASSO, SINGH\par \par \uc2 FOOTBALL, EAGLES, HURTS, VIKINGS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TIMING THE RAIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING OVERNIGHT: ON STRIKE\par \par \uc2 GENERAL MOTORS\uc2 , BARRA, \uc2 UAW\uc2 , WALKOUT, STRIKE, NEGOTIATIONS\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, George, but first, let's get to more on our top story. The United Autoworkers going on strike, taking unprecedented action again all big three automakers at once. Joining us now is \uc2 Mary Barra\uc2 , the CEO of \uc2 General Motors\uc2 . Miss Barra, thank you so much for joining us. You are in the midst of a strike and you at GM, you said your final offer for what you called, was called unprecedented is a raise of 20 percent over the life of the contract, including 10 percent in the first year. Were you surprised the union rejected it? And is that offer still on the table?\par \par \uc2 MARY BARRA (CEO \uc2 GENERAL MOTORS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Well, it's great to be here today. And, you know, as we look at the situation we're in, I'm extremely disappointed and frustrated that we're on strike because when you talk about the offer that you just reviewed, the offer is still on the table, and it's a very strong offer, not only from a gross wage perspective, the 20 percent that compounds to 21 percent over the life of the agreement, but also maintaining world class health care and job security because of the allocations we've made to all of these plants. They have product and components to continue to build, so disappointed where we're at, but the deal is still on the table and we are still at the table ready and waiting to negotiate to get people back to work.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And the union's asking for 40 percent hike in wages, which it says matches the pay increases that you and your fellow big three CEO's received over the last four years. The union said that worker salaries went up just over six percent over that same time period. Is that accurate and fair?\par \par \uc2 MARY BARRA (CEO \uc2 GENERAL MOTORS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Well, I don't know where the 40 percent came from. I will tell you from an executive compensation perspective, 92 percent of my compensation is based on performance, so when we look at where we are from a GM perspective, when GM does well, our employees do well. We've had record profit sharing over the last several years that our employees have participated in. And now in addition, we're putting a 20 percent gross wage rate increase, along with other bonuses that come as a part of that. So, this is a very, very strong financial offer, again, along with other world class benefits and health care. And the fact, and I think what our employees, is most important to them is job security, and that's important when we are in the middle of a transformation from internal combustion engine vehicles to electric vehicles and also a very competitive industry.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, with the big transformation to go ev. And, you know, last year the companies adjusted earnings before interest and tack was a record $14.5 billion. You talked about profit sharing, but profit sharing with your workers was just $500 million. They say you can afford to pay them more, so what's your response to that?\par \par \uc2 MARY BARRA (CEO \uc2 GENERAL MOTORS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Well, again, each of our employees got over $12,750 from a profit sharing perspective. I think that's significant, but from a profit perspective, everybody has to understand, the profits that \uc2 General Motors\uc2  makes we reinvest in the business, we reinvest in our people to put new products because having products that people want to buy is the way that we keep all of our assembly plants running. So we've been investing in our people, in our facilities to make sure we have a strong portfolio, so we maintain the leadership position we have in this country. \uc2 General Motors\uc2  sells more vehicles than anyone else.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Adn you talked about your transformation over from, to EVs from combustion, but that's another concern among union members because they're concerned that those cars may require fewer workers to build.\par \par \uc2 MARY BARRA (CEO \uc2 GENERAL MOTORS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Well, at \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , I know there's a lot of people who are making different assumptions or statements about the transition from ev to av. One of the things \uc2 General Motors\uc2  has done, though, because we want to make sure we take all of our employees along with us in this transformation, is we developed power units internally and we're now allocating them to the plants that build today's internal combustion engine vehicles, so we have a very concentrated plan that we've worked on for several years now to make sure that we have work for all of our employees as we make this transformation.\par \par \uc2 MARY BARRA (CEO \uc2 GENERAL MOTORS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 You know, our employees are very talented. The work that they did through covid, the work and how they manage the semiconductor shortage and supply chain challenges we still see today. They're world class and, in fact, for the last two years, \uc2 General Motors\uc2  has been recognized as having industry leading quality by jd Power. That's a result of the talented team that we have on the manufacturing floor.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, and I can say they definitely are talented and they're the best at what they do. But it's not just GM that's affected, Uaw is doing something it's never done before, striking at all three major automakers at the same time, but it said it's only going to walk out of select plants, so how will that impact your business? And will it keep me from keeping, will it be able to keep operations running? Will you be able to keep them running?\par \par \uc2 MARY BARRA (CEO \uc2 GENERAL MOTORS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Well, right now the plant that is on strike is our Wentzville plant and they build the Chevrolet Colorado and the GMC Canyon, these are brand new midsize trucks that are doing very well in the market place, as well as our cargo van that is also in strong demand, as well, so, you know, this can have an impact in a matter of days, but, right now, our other plants are running.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Will you be back at the bargaining table today?\par \par \uc2 MARY BARRA (CEO \uc2 GENERAL MOTORS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 We're there now. We're ready and want to get back to it. We want to problem solve, we want to get people back to work as quickly as possible.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, we hope that that is the case and that it happens asap, as you said. Miss Barra, thank you so much for joining us. And we want to say that \uc2 Mary Barra\uc2  is a member of the \uc2 Disney\uc2  Board, \uc2 Disney\uc2  is an ABC News parent company. Rebecca.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA MORNING MENU\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 8:11 MOMS REJOINING THE WORKFORCE\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Michael, thank you. And coming up in our "gma" Morning Menu, more moms are looking to get back into the workforce. These are the steps you should take and how to use your time away from work to your advantage.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 8:14 DANGEROUS WEIGHT LOSS TREND\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Also this morning, the new warning about what some on social media wrongly call budget Ozempic. Doctor Ashton is here with what you need to know about this dangerous trend.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 8:15 SURVIVING AGAINST THE ODDS\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Plus, meet the preemie twins who beat the odds after being born at just 22 weeks.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 8:21 DEALS AND STEALS!\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Tory Johnson back with even more "Deals & Steals" to celebrate her birthday. It's all coming up on "gma."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA, FRIDAY, SEPT 15\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020231002ej9f0000j\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 UAW STRIKE AGAINST BIG 3 AUTOMAKERS THOUSANDS OF UNION WORKERS STAGE HISTORIC WALKOUT\b0\par\par\uc2 1,017 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 September 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 UAW\uc2 , PROTESTS, BIDEN, FAIN, TRUMP, BARRA, MAKLED\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good evening. It's great to have you with us, as we near the end of another week together. Bracing for Hurricane Lee tonight in parts of the northeast. Rob is standing by.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But we begin tonight with this historic strike. For the first time ever, autoworkers going on strike against all of the big three big automakers all at once.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UAW STRIKE AGAINST BIG 3 AUTOMAKERS\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, the first three plants hit, the first three states affected. Ford just announcing 600 layoffs, they say a consequence of this strike. You're looking at pictures right now, union members rallying in downtown Detroit as we come on. They are asking for a 40% pay increase over four years. They point to CEO pay and the increasing profits for automakers. On this first day of the strike, some 2,700 workers walking out. Those three plants, they threaten to expand the strike unless their demands are met. President Biden tonight siding with the autoworkers, saying the companies should share their record profits with those workers.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ABC's Terry Moran leading us off tonight in Wayne, Michigan.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THOUSANDS OF UNION WORKERS STAGE HISTORIC WALKOUT\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Union.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, solidarity in Detroit. The \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  holding a downtown rally after launching a historic strike, targeting all of the big three US carmakers.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN (PRESIDENT UNITE AUTOMOBILE WORKERS)\par \par \uc2 This what happens when corporations don't take care of the people.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) When their contract expired at midnight with no new deal, 12,700 workers walked off the job at three auto industry facilities, a Ford plant here in Michigan, a \uc2 GM\uc2  plant in Missouri, and a Chrysler Stellantis plant in Ohio. The \uc2 UAW\uc2  says the strike will expand to other locations if its demands aren't met.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 I mean, the companies are making record profits. And it's not like we want all of it, we just would like to be able to support our families.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) At the White House today, President Biden, a longtime labor ally, openly sided with those autoworkers, a rare step for presidents, calling on the car companies to share more wealth with the workers.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Record corporate profits, which they have, should be shared by record contracts for the \uc2 UAW\uc2 . And just as we're building an economy of the future, we need labor agreements for the future.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In an interview with NBC's "Meet the Press," Republican frontrunner \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had sharp words for the head of the \uc2 UAW\uc2  who had said a second Trump term would be a disaster.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 I think he's not doing a good job representing his union because he's not going to have a union in three years from now. Those jobs are all going to be gone, because all of those electric cars are going to be made in China.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It's a fight over wages, pension, health care, and work hours. Union members want a 40% pay increase over the next four years, like their CEO's have gotten. Autoworkers saw just a 6% pay increase since the last contract. Like so many workers, they feel like they're falling behind. Several workers striking at this Ford plant telling me, they can't buy the vehicles they build.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 You don't even see a lot of the trucks that we make here in our parking lot.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You're making Broncos.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 We're making Broncos.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Rangers.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS) (CONTINUED)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Ford and \uc2 GM\uc2  are offering a 20% wage increase, but that's just half of what the union wants. \uc2 GM\uc2 's CEO, \uc2 Mary Barra\uc2 , who is on the board of directors at the \uc2 Walt Disney Company\uc2 , the parent company of ABC News, telling "GMA," her company is facing tough competition as it transitions to electric vehicles.\par \par \uc2 MARY BARRA (CEO \uc2 GENERAL MOTORS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Everybody has to understand, the profits that \uc2 General Motors\uc2  makes, we reinvest in the business. We reinvest in our people to put new products, because having products that people want to buy is the way that we keep all of our assembly plants running.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And late today, the first impact of the strike. Ford announcing it's laying off 600 workers, saying it's "a consequence of the strike at the Michigan assembly plant's final assembly and paint departments, because the components built by those employees use materials that must be e-coated for protection." The \uc2 UAW\uc2  strike follows major strikes this summer by writers and actors in Hollywood, \uc2 UPS\uc2  drivers and more. The men and women on strike here sense the long tide running against workers for decades may at last be turning.\par \par \uc2 JAY MAKLED (\uc2 UAW\uc2  LOCAL 600)\par \par \uc2 Unionization has a momentum right now that we feel that workers are demanding more.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, let's bring back in Terry Moran again tonight, this time outside that Ford plant in Wayne, Michigan. And, Terry, the White House now is planning to send a team to Detroit?\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's right, David. The President is sending the acting labor secretary Julie Su and Gene Sperling, a top economic adviser, here to try to bring the parties back to the table and get a deal. Combined with the President's pro-union statement today, it shows he's taking an activist role in this dispute, and that's a sign of the high economic and political stakes here. David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Terry Moran live in Wayne, Michigan tonight. Terry, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020230919ej9f00002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 WORLD NEWS TONIGHT WITH DAVID MUIR\b0\par\par\uc2 560 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 September 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Tonight, breaking news. For the first time ever, thousands of \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  on strike against the big three automakers all at once. Also, tonight in the northeast, bracing for Hurricane Lee tonight. And the officer heard laughing on body cam hours after responding to the death of a pedestrian in a police collision.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UAW STRIKE AGAINST BIG 3 AUTOMAKERS\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 First, tonight, the \uc2 UAW\uc2  is holding a large rally at this hour, more than 12,000 workers walking off the job. The union demanding a 40% pay hike, pointing to record profits for the automakers. The three plants hit first across three states. President Biden tonight siding with the autoworkers. Former President Trump critical, though, of union leadership. Tonight, news coming in. Ford laying off hundreds 600 workers, calling it a consequence of the strike. Terry Moran with late reporting. He's standing by in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HURRICANE LEE LASHES EAST COAST\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Hurricane Lee nearing the East Coast tonight. Tropical storm warnings up for parts of the northeast at this hour. States of emergency already in effect. Senior meteorologist Rob Marciano standing by to time this out.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SPECIAL COUNSEL REQUESTS TRUMP GAG ORDER\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 There is late word tonight, special counsel Jack Smith is now requesting the judge in the January 6th case issue a narrow gag order against former President Trump, accusing him of making intimidating statements targeting potential witnesses. Pierre Thomas with late reporting.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VIDEO: OFFICER LAUGHS ABOUT FATAL ACCIDENT\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Disturbing police video igniting outrage in Seattle tonight. An officer under investigation for what he said about a woman struck and killed by another police car. The officer can be heard laughing and saying her life had, quote, "limited value."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CYBERATTACKS TARGETING LAS VEGAS RESORTS\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 From Atlantic City to Las Vegas, the casinos and the cyberattack, machines going dark. And tonight, the threat has now intensified. What they're threatening now with screens dark. Our reporter witnessing casino workers now paying customers by hand. Mola Lenghi on the casino floor tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEATH TOLL SOARING\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Stunning new images coming in of the devastating flooding in Libya. A wall of water, 23 feet high, sweeping through the city. Tonight, officials now saying more than 11,000 people have been killed.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MIDAIR SCARE AT 37,000 FEET\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Tonight, back here in the US, the midair air scare for United passengers leaving from Newark Airport, suddenly descending nearly 30,000 feet in a matter of minutes. Concern over cabin pressure, the plane then coming back.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RODGERS: "WATCH WHAT I DO"\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we hear from Aaron Rodgers, for the first time, days after tearing his Achilles tendon, while millions watch on "Monday Night Football." What he's now saying about returning this season. The very personal news revealed by actor Hugh Jackman today. And later here tonight, they are the unsung heroes at school. Tonight, though, a dream fulfilled. So, who is our "Person of the Week"?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WORLD NEWS TONIGHT WITH DAVID MUIR\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 From ABC News world headquarters in New York, this is ABC "World News Tonight with David Muir."\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020230919ej9f00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 WOMEN IN THE WORKPLACE REACH NEW MILESTONE NUMBER OF WORKING MOMS IN US LABOR FORCE\b0\par\par\uc2 1,058 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 September 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 HURRICANE LEE, WEATHER, TRACK\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, HURRICANE LEE\par \par \uc2 CAVALCANTE, RYAN, CLARK, BIVENS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ESCAPED KILLER BACK IN CUSTODY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOW AUTHORITIES FINALLY CAUGHT HIM AFTER TWO WEEKS ON THE RUN\par \par \uc2 PROTESTS, \uc2 UAW\uc2 , FAIN, ROSSI, SKONIECZNY, FARLEY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CLOCK TICKING TO AUTOWORKERS STRIKE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UNION PRESIDENT UNVEILS WALKOUT AS MIDNIGHT DEADLINE NEARS\par \par \uc2 LIBYA, FLOODING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FLOODING CATASTROPHE IN LIBYA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEATH TOLL RISES TO AT LEAST 5,300 WITH MORE THAN 7,000 INJURED\par \par \uc2 RESCUE, CRUISE SHIP, GREENLAND\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LUXURY CRUISE SHIP STRANDED\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RACE TO REACH STUCK VESSEL WITH MORE THAN 200 PEOPLE ONBOARD\par \par \uc2 ROMNEY, RETIREMENT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MITT ROMNEY TO RETIRE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UTAH SENATOR WILL NOT RUN FOR REELECTION, CALLS FOR "NEW GENERATION OF LEADERS"\par \par \uc2 RODGERS, INJURY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIGHT NOW\par \par \uc2 KOHBERGER, TRIAL\par \par \uc2 DICKEY, RESCUE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RESCUED AMERICAN CAVER SPEAKS OUT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MARK DICKEY ON HIS HARROWING ORDEAL & HOW HE'S DOING NOW\par \par \uc2 HURRICANE LEE, TRACK, PARRISH, ABITBOL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: INTO THE HURRICANE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ON BOARD WITH HURRICANE HUNTERS AS THEY FLY INTO CENTER OF LEE\par \par \uc2 VMA, NSYNC\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 LEE, HURRICANE, STORM, WATCH, WEATHER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HURRICANE LEE ON THE MOVE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LATEST TRACKING & TIMING OF STORM\par \par \uc2 UNITED AUTOWORKERS, BIG THREE, STRIKE, DEADLINE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CLOCK TICKING TO AUTOWORKERS STRIKE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UNION PRESIDENT UNVEILS WALKOUT PLAN\par \par \uc2 WATER BEADS, BUFFALO GAMES, INFANT, DEATH, RECALL\par \par \uc2 WORKING, PARENTHOOD, FLEXIBILITY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WOMEN IN THE WORKPLACE REACH NEW MILESTONE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NUMBER OF WORKING MOMS IN US LABOR FORCE\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we are back with our "GMA" cover story. We're kicking off a new series on working moms. Rebecca, you found women have reached a new milestone in the workplace.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It is an important milestone, George, thank you, and it is part and due to the strong jobs market and the rise of remote work. The number of moms in the labor force with young children is now at an all-time high.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It's been a record-breaking summer for women in the workforce with the participation rate for those between the ages of 25 and 54 hitting never before seen heights, a stark contrast to the troves of women who left the workforce during the height of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 KAREN KIMBROUGH (LINKEDIN)\par \par \uc2 We're seeing an increase in hybrid work. We see that women apply to remote work more than men do, and in that case, flexibility really becomes, I think, the lynchpin for getting women back into the labor force.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STRONG JOBS MARKET & REMOTE WORK INCREASING OPPORTUNITIES\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Flexibility was crucial for Anne Louis, who returned to work about a year after leaving her job following the premature birth of her son, who needs special care.\par \par \uc2 ANNE LOUIS (WORKING MOM)\par \par \uc2 I needed a company that was willing to adjust if I had big emergencies. I had to position myself to have the flexibility and have the income enough to invest in my son's future. So, I was very transparent about how much money I wanted to get paid. I was very transparent about what I needed in terms of flexibility.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Among women returning to the workforce, those with young children are leading the trend, in part due to more flexible options, as well as inflation and rising childcare costs. But there are also more opportunities. There are still more jobs than job seekers, with roles in customer service, sales and nursing topping the list for most in demand.\par \par \uc2 KAREN KIMBROUGH (LINKEDIN)\par \par \uc2 There's a lot of flexibility in sectors that provide consulting, accounting, bookkeeping services. So, I would encourage people to think not just in the sector that traditionally seems where women in clusters, but looking across all sectors, there's a lot of opportunity for hybrid and remote work.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Anne said the job flexibility that's allowed her household a dual income has been essential to providing the best care and education for her son.\par \par \uc2 ANNE LOUIS (WORKING MOM)\par \par \uc2 Without the flexibility, there is no resources. Without the income, there were no resources. There was no extra help. There wouldn't be an opportunity for my son to go to one of the best schools that he goes to now. I get to be a part of giving him the world because the world was not promised to him at one point.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Rebecca, having more women in the workforce, critical for the economy.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Absolutely critical, George. It's critical for the bottom line of the United States of America. It means more tax revenue, it means more spending power. Our economy is powered by consumers. Two-thirds of the economy is consumer spending. More women working means more money in the economy, and this is why people are paying such close attention to this question of childcare, because we've seen all of these gains since the pandemic, but this childcare issue is still something that keeps women out.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOW TO NEGOTIATE FOR REMOTE OR FLEXIBLE JOB OPPORTUNITIES\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) How can working moms who need the flexibility negotiate it?\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's so important to have the conversation up front. That's what Anne did. When you are negotiating for a new job, that's the time you have the best power to get exactly what you want out of that employer. LinkedIn also recommends that you let the company know what the benefit is going to be to them. So, it's not just, "I need this for me," it's, "I'm going to save time on my commute. I can work from home and be more accessible to you as an employee." And then finally, what you want to do is offer a trial period. If the employer is still pushing back, say, "Why don't we do this for two weeks and just see how it goes?" And if you can make it work for those two weeks, you can make it work for the whole thing.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, interesting stuff. Looking forward to more of the series.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020230929ej9e0003d\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 CLOCK TICKING TO AUTOWORKERS STRIKE UNION PRESIDENT UNVEILS WALKOUT AS MIDNIGHT DEADLINE NEARS\b0\par\par\uc2 576 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 September 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 HURRICANE LEE, WEATHER, TRACK\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, HURRICANE LEE\par \par \uc2 CAVALCANTE, RYAN, CLARK, BIVENS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ESCAPED KILLER BACK IN CUSTODY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOW AUTHORITIES FINALLY CAUGHT HIM AFTER TWO WEEKS ON THE RUN\par \par \uc2 PROTESTS, \uc2 UAW\uc2 , FAIN, ROSSI, SKONIECZNY, FARLEY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CLOCK TICKING TO AUTOWORKERS STRIKE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UNION PRESIDENT UNVEILS WALKOUT AS MIDNIGHT DEADLINE NEARS\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Meantime the clock is ticking on a potentially unprecedented strike by the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  against all of the big three automakers at the same time.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The union's president is talking tough, promising walkouts if there's no deal by midnight. Senior national correspondent Terry Moran has the latest from Detroit. Good morning, Terry.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Good morning, Rebecca. Just hours before that midnight deadline and workers here are bracing for it and they're preparing for it. They're getting their assignments to walk the picket lines, preparing strike pantries for families who may need assistance and they're telling us they are ready for this moment.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, the American auto industry is on the brink of an unprecedented strike that could have major consequences for the entire US economy.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN (PRESIDENT \uc2 UAW\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 For the first time in our history, we may strike all three of the big three at once.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 We are the union.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 ' president announcing plans to go out on strike after weeks of negotiations between the union and Ford, \uc2 GM\uc2  and Chrysler-owner \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  have so far failed to reach a deal. Workers are poised to walk out at specific targeted plants of all three major automakers, a huge step.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Who are we?\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 UAW\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Among the workers' demand, a 40% wage increase over the next four years, with the union arguing that's what the CEOs have gotten since the last contract, a return to pension plans eliminated during the financial crisis, retiree health care and a four-day work week.\par \par \uc2 PAT ROSSI (\uc2 UAW\uc2  WORKER)\par \par \uc2 It looks on paper like we're asking for a lot, but a lot of people have been left behind.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Union members pointing to a decade of record profits for the automakers and to those huge CEO salaries.\par \par \uc2 GREG SKONIECZNY (\uc2 UAW\uc2  WORKER)\par \par \uc2 I don't mind them making that money, but I think we need to take care of the employees.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But the companies say the workers' demands are unrealistic especially as the industry tries to transition to electric vehicles.\par \par \uc2 JIM FARLEY\uc2  CEO FORD\par \par \uc2 We're literally fighting for the future of the auto manufacturing in our country. But we're optimistic we'll find a way forward.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Workers here tell us they're anxious, several telling us nobody wins in a strike. But they're also determined. They want to seize this moment. They believe that this is the time to reset the balance between workers and management, which has gone out so far out of whack in the past few decades. And not just in the auto industry. Michael?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Terry, we hope they can make, they can work it out for sure. Thank you so much for that.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020230929ej9e0002x\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 CLOCK TICKING TO AUTOWORKERS STRIKE UNION PRESIDENT UNVEILS WALKOUT PLAN\b0\par\par\uc2 532 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 September 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 HURRICANE LEE, WEATHER, TRACK\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, HURRICANE LEE\par \par \uc2 CAVALCANTE, RYAN, CLARK, BIVENS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ESCAPED KILLER BACK IN CUSTODY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOW AUTHORITIES FINALLY CAUGHT HIM AFTER TWO WEEKS ON THE RUN\par \par \uc2 PROTESTS, \uc2 UAW\uc2 , FAIN, ROSSI, SKONIECZNY, FARLEY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CLOCK TICKING TO AUTOWORKERS STRIKE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UNION PRESIDENT UNVEILS WALKOUT AS MIDNIGHT DEADLINE NEARS\par \par \uc2 LIBYA, FLOODING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FLOODING CATASTROPHE IN LIBYA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEATH TOLL RISES TO AT LEAST 5,300 WITH MORE THAN 7,000 INJURED\par \par \uc2 RESCUE, CRUISE SHIP, GREENLAND\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LUXURY CRUISE SHIP STRANDED\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RACE TO REACH STUCK VESSEL WITH MORE THAN 200 PEOPLE ONBOARD\par \par \uc2 ROMNEY, RETIREMENT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MITT ROMNEY TO RETIRE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UTAH SENATOR WILL NOT RUN FOR REELECTION, CALLS FOR "NEW GENERATION OF LEADERS"\par \par \uc2 RODGERS, INJURY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIGHT NOW\par \par \uc2 KOHBERGER, TRIAL\par \par \uc2 DICKEY, RESCUE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RESCUED AMERICAN CAVER SPEAKS OUT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MARK DICKEY ON HIS HARROWING ORDEAL & HOW HE'S DOING NOW\par \par \uc2 HURRICANE LEE, TRACK, PARRISH, ABITBOL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: INTO THE HURRICANE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ON BOARD WITH HURRICANE HUNTERS AS THEY FLY INTO CENTER OF LEE\par \par \uc2 VMA, NSYNC\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 LEE, HURRICANE, STORM, WATCH, WEATHER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HURRICANE LEE ON THE MOVE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LATEST TRACKING & TIMING OF STORM\par \par \uc2 UNITED AUTOWORKERS, BIG THREE, STRIKE, DEADLINE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CLOCK TICKING TO AUTOWORKERS STRIKE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UNION PRESIDENT UNVEILS WALKOUT PLAN\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to the latest on a possible strike by the United Autoworkers against all of the big three automakers at the same time. The union's president is promising walkouts if there's no deal by midnight deadlines. Let's go back to Terry Moran in Detroit. Good morning again, Terry.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Michael. That clock is ticking, and the union workers are poised to do something they have never done in American history, strike all three of the big US automakers. They are poised to do that here at this Ford plant behind me and at plants across the Midwest. So, this could have major impacts on the US economy. But the workers we talked to, Michael, they seem determined. We were at a local union yesterday, as people came in to get their assignments to walk the picket lines and provide support to families who may need assistance during a strike, and the mood was really both anxious and hopeful, with more than one worker telling us nobody wins during a strike, but it is time to change things.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, the workers are demanding a 40 percent pay increase over four years. Now, that sounds like an awful lot, but the union argues that's what the CEOs have been giving themselves over the past several years, since the last contract. And the workers also want pension benefits lost during the financial crisis restored, as well as retiree health care benefits. Talks continued well into the night, and both sides are saying progress is being made, but they still remain far apart, with union president Shawn Fain telling his workers that it looks like they will have to take action. So, be prepared, Rebecca.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) High stakes with that clock ticking. Okay, Terry, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020230929ej9e0003b\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 AUTOWORKERS STRIKE DEADLINE UNION EMPLOYEES SET TO BEGIN STRIKE AT MIDNIGHT\b0\par\par\uc2 607 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 September 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 BIDEN, INDICTMENT, GARLAND\par \par \uc2 HURRICANE LEE, WEATHER, STORM, TRACK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HURRICANE LEE THREATENS NEW ENGLAND\par \par \uc2 PROTESTS, \uc2 UAW\uc2 , FAIN, MAKLED, FARLEY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AUTOWORKERS STRIKE DEADLINE\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We continue with the other news tonight, and to late developments in the faceoff between the \uc2 United Auto Workers union\uc2  and the big three automakers in this country. The deadline for a strike now just hours away. The union tonight saying they have failed to reach an agreement and that they could walk beginning at midnight. If so, the first time the union has struck all three companies at once. ABC's Terry Moran in Dearborn, Michigan, tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UNION EMPLOYEES SET TO BEGIN STRIKE AT MIDNIGHT\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, nearly 150,000 American autoworkers say they are ready to walk off the job at midnight in the first ever strike against all of the big three US automakers.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN (PRESIDENT UNITED AUTO WORKERS)\par \par \uc2 We are preparing to strike these companies in a way they've never seen before.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The \uc2 UAW\uc2  is turning to new tactics to ramp up the pressure, targeted strikes to disrupt operations at certain plants. Tonight, there is still no deal in sight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMANDS\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Workers want a 40% increase in wages over the next four years, in line with the 40% increase they say their CEOs have made since the last contract. And they want pensions, better retiree health care, and cost of living adjustments. The autoworkers at UAW Local 600 build the Ford 150 truck.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You guys are building like the most popular vehicle in America.\par \par \uc2 JAY MAKLED (\uc2 UAW\uc2  LOCAL 600)\par \par \uc2 Number one selling truck in the world. Our members here work 11 hours a day, six to seven days a week, building the most iconic vehicle for \uc2 Ford Motor Company\uc2 . And, you know, they want a fair share in the profit.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Those big three profits are soaring, $21 billion in just the first six months of this year. We asked Ford's CEO about something workers bring up a lot, his $20 million pay package.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They're asking about CEO pay. You're the CEO, made $20 million, and they say a 40% increase since the last contract. Why can't they get a 40% increase?\par \par \uc2 JIM FARLEY\uc2  (CEO OF FORD)\par \par \uc2 Well, I think, first of all, we are offering an incredible unprecedented increase. But we can't even get any feedback from the \uc2 UAW\uc2  about what they want. All they want is for everyone to make $300,000. That's not sustainable. We'll go bankrupt if we do that.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Ford insists the company can't afford the total compensation package the union wants for its workers. Ford and \uc2 GM\uc2  are out with new offers, a 20% wage increase, just half of what the union is looking for.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) David, if there's a strike tonight, and it lasts for a few weeks, there are ten states with major auto industry employees that would get hit hard with Michigan getting hit first and hardest. Up to 300,000 people here potentially without a paycheck. But other states, Ohio, Texas and Illinois, Kentucky, Indiana, Kansas, Tennessee, even New York, all have auto industry employees. This would have major regional, national economic impacts. David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A broad impact indeed. Terry Moran with us again tonight on this. Terry, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020230918ej9e00004\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 UAW\uc2  PRESIDENT ON LOOMING AUTOWORKERS STRIKE LATEST ON STATE OF THE NEGOTIATIONS AS DEADLINE NEARS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,048 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 September 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FACE TO FACE\par \par \uc2 KIM, PUTIN, MEETING, UKRAINE, AMMUNITION\par \par \uc2 STORM, RAIN, LEE, WIND, RIP CURRENTS, WAVES\par \par \uc2 FLOODING, LIBYA, DEATHS, MISSING, RESIDENTS\par \par \uc2 MANHUNT, CAVALCANTE, BIVENS, GUN, THERIAULT, SHAPIRO\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY, BIDEN, IMPEACHMENT, VOTE, HOUSE\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY, IMPEACHMENT, BIDEN, WHITE HOUSE\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS, MEMPHIS, DOJ, INDICTED, GARLAND, CLARKE\par \par \uc2 INFLATION, REPORT, INCREASE, FED, INTEREST RATES\par \par \uc2 RODGERS, JETS, ACHILLES, SEASON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 VAN ORD, DICKEY, TRAPPED, RESCUE, CAVE, ILLNESS\par \par \uc2 FAIN, AUTOWORKERS, STRIKE, AUTOMAKERS, PAY, CEOS\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We are back with the latest on the threatened strike by the United Autoworkers against the big three automakers. A strike by the 150,000 members against all three companies would be unprecedented. As the clock tick downs to a Midnight deadline tomorrow, we're joined by the \uc2 UAW\uc2  President, Shawn Fain. Mr. Fain, thank you for joining us this morning. Is there going to be a strike?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 UAW\uc2  PRESIDENT ON LOOMING AUTOWORKERS STRIKE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LATEST ON STATE OF NEGOTIATIONS AS DEADLINE NEARS\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN (PRESIDENT UNITED AUTO WORKERS)\par \par \uc2 Thank you, George. That's up to the companies. You know, just in the past, you know, we've been very clear with the company here recently that September 14th is a deadline, not a reference point. And, you know, we've done things very differently this round of bargaining. We've told the companies up front. We expect our members' demands to get addressed. We gave it to them right at the outset of bargaining rather than wait until the end.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN (PRESIDENT UNITED AUTO WORKERS)\par \par \uc2 We told the companies don't wait until the end. We want to get a good agreement for our members. That's what we, that's been the goal. We've been at the table every day waiting. Unfortunately we had to file unfair labor charges against two of the companies just to get them to the table. And so, you know, unfortunately, they've waited until the last week to get serious about bargaining. So we got a lot of work to do in the next 48 hours.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well the White House says they're optimistic. \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  says there's been progress. You've changed your tune a little bit. Last week you called their offers insulting. Yesterday you said there was progress. Are you still making progress?\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN (PRESIDENT UNITED AUTO WORKERS)\par \par \uc2 We're making progress but it's slow. And, you know, the clock's ticking. You know, as we've told these companies, you know, tick tock, you got to watch that clock and it's gonna run out. And that's what happens when you wait until the last minute. So, can we get there? Yes, we can, but they need to buckle down and get busy. We've got 48 hours, and that's not a lot of time to go the distance we need to go.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It does sound, though, relatively encouraging. If there is a strike, would it be against all three automakers?\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN (PRESIDENT UNITED AUTO WORKERS)\par \par \uc2 And, again, we've been very up front from the beginning with the companies that, you know, we're not picking a target. The target is the big three. And we expect, you know, September 14th is the deadline. We expect to have agreements with all three companies by that date. The workers deserve that. And if they do not have an agreement by that date, then there will be action.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) How concerned are you that a strike could affect the broader economy and end up hurting more than it helps?\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN (PRESIDENT UNITED AUTO WORKERS)\par \par \uc2 Well, you know, it's interesting to me that all of a sudden the fear mongers get out there and they start talking about, you know, how this is going to wreck the economy. It's not going to wreck the economy. It's going to wreck the billionaire economy. And that's the big issue here, you know, especially in this country. You know, the billionaire class is running away with everything. The working class is being left living paycheck to paycheck and feeding off the scraps.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN (PRESIDENT UNITED AUTO WORKERS)\par \par \uc2 These corporations have made a quarter of a trillion dollars in the last decade, $21 billion in the last six months alone. You know, the price of vehicles went up 30% in the last four years. CEO pay went up 40% in the last four years. Worker pay went up 6%. We're not the problem. Labor is less than 10% of the cost of the vehicle. And so when the companies talk about competition and all these, all these key words, it's code for race to the bottom. That's what they're trying to do is drive a race to the bottom. And the workers deserve better than that.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Expect to be talking directly to the companies today?\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN (PRESIDENT UNITED AUTO WORKERS)\par \par \uc2 I'm sorry?\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Will you be talking directly to the companies today?\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN (PRESIDENT UNITED AUTO WORKERS)\par \par \uc2 Yes, sir. We have meetings. We're open. We've been here every day. We were here late last night until the Midnight hour. And we've been available every day for the last seven to eight weeks. Unfortunately, you know, the companies are just now choosing to get serious about this. So we'll see where we end up. But we're available. We plan on meeting at 10:00 AM this morning with one of them, and we'll keep moving from there.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thanks for your time this morning.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN (PRESIDENT UNITED AUTO WORKERS)\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Linsey.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BRIDESMAID BURNOUT\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, George. Coming up, bridesmaid burnout. How to manage the high cost of so many friends getting married?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And next, Taylor Swift, Shakira and a surprise boy band reunion. It's our VMAs "Play of the Day."\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020230929ej9d0000e\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 AUTOWORKERS PREPARE TO STRIKE TENS OF THOUSANDS THREATENING TO WALK OUT\b0\par\par\uc2 537 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 September 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 CAVALCANTE, ARREST, BIVENS, RYAN, MARTIN, BOYD\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, HURRICANE LEE, FLOODING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HURRICANE WATCHES ON NEW ENGLAND COAST\par \par \uc2 CRUISE SHIP, GREENLAND, RESCUE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CRUISE SHIP STRANDED IN GREENLAND\par \par \uc2 ROMNEY, RE-ELECTION, POLITICS, AGE, TRUMP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ROMNEY: WILL NOT SEEK RE-ELECTION\par \par \uc2 LIBYA, FLOODING, EARTHQUAKE, SEARCH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FLOOD DEATHS OVER 6,000\par \par \uc2 PUTIN, KIM, NORTH KOREA, RUSSIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PUTIN-KIM SPACEPORT SUMMIT\par \par \uc2 UAW\uc2 , PROTEST, REISS, SKONIECZNY, FARLEY, FAIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AUTOWORKERS PREPARE TO STRIKE\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Back here in the US now, and what the \uc2 UAW\uc2  tonight just signaled a short time ago as the big three automakers brace for a possible strike. The strike deadline, a little more than 24 hours away now. So, what we've learned tonight from Terry Moran in Detroit.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TENS OF THOUSANDS THREATENING TO WALK OUT\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, the clock is ticking down to a deadline of midnight tomorrow for a threatened strike by nearly 150,000 autoworkers.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Who are we?\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 UAW\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) For rank-and-file members, a moment of truth.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) How do you feel at this moment, the deadline just a few hours away?\par \par \uc2 DERRICK REISS (UAW MEMBER)\par \par \uc2 Anxious. Hopeful.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) If no deal is reached, it would mark the first time in history that union workers walk out on all of the big three American automakers. The \uc2 UAW\uc2  wants a 40% wage increase over the next four years, arguing that's how much the CEOs have gotten since the last contract.\par \par \uc2 GREG SKONIECZNY (UAW MEMBER)\par \par \uc2 I don't mind them making all that money. That's great. But don't take away from me to pad your own pocket.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Other union demands, a return to pension plans, which were eliminated for new hires during the financial crisis, plus retiree health care, and a four-day work week. A demand Ford's CEO says is unrealistic.\par \par \uc2 JIM FARLEY\uc2  (CEO FORD)\par \par \uc2 We'll see. I mean, a four-day work week is not attainable.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) As for worries that a major auto strike could hurt the US economy, \uc2 UAW\uc2  president Shawn fain had this to say just moments ago\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN (UAW PRESIDENT)\par \par \uc2 It's not the economy it will wreck, it's their economy, it's the billionaire economy. That's what they're worried about.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) At this hour, the talks continue. Both sides saying progress has been made, but they remain far apart. And \uc2 UAW\uc2  president Fain telling his members tonight, to win, we are likely going to have to take action. David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Terry Moran watching just outside Detroit tonight. Terry, thank you. When we come back here, the member of Congress, her husband killed in a plane crash. And what we're learning about his fin moments at the controls. Also, the former \uc2 NBA\uc2  player who collapsed and died during yoga. And after what happened to Aaron Rodgers this week, the push tonight for a big change on the field.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020230915ej9d00008\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 LOOMING STRIKE AGAINST BIG THREE AUTOMAKERS UNION WORKERS SEEKING 32-HOUR WORK WEEK AND 40% RAISE\b0\par\par\uc2 594 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 September 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 EARTHQUAKE, SEARCH, RESCUE, MOROCCO\par \par \uc2 HURRICANE LEE, WEATHER, TRACK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HURRICANE LEE MAINTAINING STRENGTH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HAZARDOUS SURF, RIP CURRENTS POSSIBLE IN DAYS AHEAD\par \par \uc2 US OPEN, GAUFF\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 19-YEAR-OLD COCO GAUFF TRIUMPHS AT U.S. OPEN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: YOUNGEST AMERICAN TO WIN TITLE SINCE SERENA WILLIAMS IN 1999\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, G20 SUMMIT, VIETNAM\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT BIDEN VISITS VIETNAM\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EXPECTED TO ANNOUNCE NEW PARTNERSHIPS IN TECH, EDUCATION & BUSINESS\par \par \uc2 ELECTION, TRUMP, DESANTIS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE RIVALS CONVERGE ON IOWA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  & RON DESANTIS ATTEND SAME FOOTBALL GAME\par \par \uc2 CAVALCANTE, MANHUNT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MASSIVE MANHUNT CONTINUES FOR ESCAPED CONVICTED KILLER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEARCH PERIMETER EXPANDS AS OFFICIALS REPORT MORE CONFIRMED SIGHTINGS\par \par \uc2 STRIKE, \uc2 UAW\uc2 , SCHAMBERS, PEDRAZA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LOOMING STRIKE AGAINST BIG THREE AUTOMAKERS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UNION WORKERS SEEKING 32-HOUR WORK WEEK AND 40% RAISE\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to that looming strike against the big three automakers.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) 150,000 autoworkers prepared to walk off the job this week. Maryalice Parks has been speaking with some of them about why they're ready for that drastic step.\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The United Autoworkers Union, and its 150,000 members still threatening to strike if the so-called big three car manufacturers do not meet demands for significant raises.\par \par \uc2 SARA SCHAMBERS (\uc2 UAW\uc2  MEMBER & WORKER \uc2 FORD MOTOR COMPANY\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 It's not we're looking to strike, it's just that we're looking for a fair contract.\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) A mother of two, she hopes to buy a house.\par \par \uc2 SARA SCHAMBERS (\uc2 UAW\uc2  MEMBER & WORKER \uc2 FORD MOTOR COMPANY\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 There's no house that would fit me and my two children that is affordable right now with the wages that I make.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 UAW UNION\uc2  DEMANDS\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The \uc2 UAW\uc2  is demanding help with inflation costs, guarantees for future union jobs and a 40% raise increase for most workers over four years. It's the latest in a growing trend of unions flexing their muscle and demanding double-digit raises for workers.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LABOR CONTRACTS RATIFIED\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This last year, UPS part-time workers, American and Delta Airline workers, and West Coast dockworkers have all demanded and secured raises over 30%. Marcie Pedraza, an electrician of almost 11 years, is a single mom.\par \par \uc2 MARCIE PEDRAZA (ELECTRICIAN CHICAGO ASSEMBLY PLANT)\par \par \uc2 As far as the members' demands are met, it would make a huge impact on quality of life for all working families.\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Friday, \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  offered over 14% raises, telling employees the offer, quote, "protects the company's future ability to continue to compete globally in an industry that is rapidly transitioning to electric vehicles." In a recent statement after making their latest public offer, \uc2 General Motors\uc2  said their goal remains the same, to achieve an agreement without a disruption. Both Ford and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  have talked about the pressures of a changing industry. Ford saying they're committed too to looking for creative solutions to reach a deal.\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's hard to gauge any potential economic impact right now. It would depend on whether the strike is against one or all three of these car manufacturers. The White House trying to stay optimistic. We are told the President is closely monitoring the situation. Stephanie?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hopefully, they can all come to an agreement this week. Thank you so much, Maryalice Parks, in Washington for us.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020230921ej9a00030\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 TIME RUNNING OUT ON AUTOMAKERS NEGOTIATIONS UNION COULD STRIKE NEXT WEEK IF TALKS FAIL\b0\par\par\uc2 365 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 September 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING OVERNIGHT: DEVASTATING EARTHQUAKE IN MOROCCO\par \par \uc2 MOROCCO, EARTHQUAKE, TREMBLER, BARNES\par \par \uc2 TEMPERATURES, HEAT, CALIFORNIA, TEXAS\par \par \uc2 ESCAPEE, MURDERER, MANHUNT, PENNSYLVANIA, SEARCH\par \par \uc2 IRIZARRY, OFFICER, VIDEO, KRASNER, MCMONAGLE, GARCIA\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, INDIA, SUMMIT, G20, UKRAINE, CLIMATE, KIRBY, GUTERRES\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, RUSSIA, WAR, ARMY, MISSILE, US\par \par \uc2 MEADOWS, COURT\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, MUGSHOT, SOUTH DAKOTA, NOEM\par \par \uc2 UAW\uc2 , AUTOMAKERS, NEGOTIATIONS, STRIKE\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Turning now to the looming strike threat by the United Autoworkers Union against the big three automakers. The Biden administration says it's closely monitoring the situation, and Maryalice Parks is joining us now from the White House with more. Maryalice, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Whit, good morning. This President likes to call himself the most pro-union President, and the line from the West Wing this week has been, we believe in collective bargaining. A senior White House aide told me that the President wants to make sure the unions knows he has their backs and that they don't feel pressured to take a deal they don't like.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 UAW\uc2  REJECTS COUNTEROFFERS OF BIG 3 AUTOMAKERS. WHITE HOUSE CLOSELY MONITORING SITUATION\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The President is closely monitoring all of this, but I do get the sense that they are trying to keep it a bit at an arm's length. Yesterday, we saw \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  that owns Chrysler present a counteroffer, they were the last of the big three manufacturers to do so. The offer was for about 14.5 percent raises for most employees over the life of these new four-year contracts, but like we saw with the other offers, the uaw President took to social media and really mocked the proposal. It was a far cry from the 40 percent raises that the union has been pushing for. And this is really just the latest showdown in what has been a trend of unions across industries flexing their muscle. September 14th, that's the deadline for these autoworkers, so, Will, the clock is ticking.\par \par \uc2 WILL REEVE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, that's soon. Maryalice, thanks for that.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020230921ej990000a\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 AUTOWORKERS STRIKE LOOMS UNION REJECTS GM'S LATEST OFFERING, CALLS IT "INSULTING"\b0\par\par\uc2 314 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 September 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HURRICANE LEE INTENSIFYING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BRUTAL HEAT\par \par \uc2 LEE, WINDS, EAST COAST, INTENSITY, TRACK\par \par \uc2 STORMS, LIGHTNING, RAIN, HAIL, POWER, HEATWAVE\par \par \uc2 MANHUNT, SEARCH, CAVALCANTE, RESIDENTS, BIVENS\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, G-20, SUMMIT, CHINA, UKRAINE VIETNAM\par \par \uc2 CONVICTED, NAVARRO, JURY, HOUSE, JAN 6TH\par \par \uc2 AUTOWORKERS, STRIKE, GM, FORD, \uc2 STELLANTIS\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, thank you, Pierre. Going to turn now to the latest on the looming autoworker strike. The union responding to GM's latest counter proposal, and it certainly was not the response the auto giant was hoping for. Elizabeth Schulze joins us now with details. Good morning, Elizabeth.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AUTOWORKERS STRIKE LOOMS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UNION REJECTS GM'S LATEST OFFERING, CALLS IT "INSULTING"\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Michael. It certainly was not. And a possible strike is now less than a week away. And there is still no deal in sight between the United Autoworkers Union and Detroit's big three automakers. GM yesterday offering a 10% pay hike to its workers, calling it the biggest raise since 1999.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But it is still far from the 40% increase the \uc2 UAW\uc2  members are demanding over four years along with better benefits and more paid time off. The union's president responding, "GM has come to the table with an insulting proposal. GM either doesn't care or isn't listening when we say we need economic justice by 11:59 PM on September 14th."\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now, at that time, if there's no agreement and the union's 146,000 factory workers go on strike, GM, Ford and \uc2 Jeep\uc2  maker \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , could be forced to slow down or stop production altogether. And if the strike goes on, that could mean fewer cars at dealers and higher prices for consumers. Rebecca.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020230919ej9800007\par }\par \b \uc2 Search Summary\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\b